## VI. Prevention — Stop Them From Recurring

### Defensive Design
| Principle | Implementation |
|---|---|
| **Idempotency** | Replay must not duplicate; use unique request IDs / upsert patterns |
| **Checkpointing** | Long jobs → persist progress; resume without re-processing everything |
| **Error Typing** | Distinguish transient vs permanent in code → auto-policy applies |
| **Circuit Breakers** | 5+ failures to same service → pause queue; prevent cascading |
| **Dead Man Switch** | Unresolved DLQ entries > threshold → escalate to on-call |

### Observability & Alerting
- **Rate of new failures** > threshold → alert
- **DLQ size growth** → alert
- **Retry success %** dropping → alert
- **Mean time to resolution (MTTR)** tracking → process improvement
- Failure reason distribution dashboard → identify hotspots

### Operational Hygiene
- Periodic DLQ review meetings → turn incidents into tests
- Add failure scenario to staging test suite
- Update retry policies as error patterns emerge
- Document "known failure + fix" runbook entries

---

## VII. Decision Matrix — Quick Reference

| Situation | Recommended Action |
|---|---|
| Network/timeout/5xx → succeeded on manual retry | Adjust retry policy; auto-retry transient |
| Bad data / validation error → same every time | Fix source → replay; add pre-validation |
| Downstream API changed → all jobs failing | Fix integration → replay all; add contract test |
| Job partially succeeded → safe to resume | Resume from checkpoint; do not re-run from start |
| Duplication risk on re-run | Idempotent replay; unique key; manual approval gate |
| Legacy/unmaintained job → unclear state | Archive; document; accept if business impact low |
| Critical path → SLA violation | Escalate; manual fix; temporary bypass + follow-up fix |

